WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.840
Part comic book, part ghost&nbsp;
story, part nightmare fuel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.260
Guillermo Del Toro's "Cabinet&nbsp;
of Curiosities" is a chilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:12.360
collection of terrifying tales – and these are&nbsp;
the scariest moments. Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.380
In "Lot 36," we meet Nick,&nbsp;
played by Tim Blake Nelson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.680
a man who makes his living buying&nbsp;
abandoned storage lockers and selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.100
their contents. After purchasing&nbsp;
a unit whose owner recently died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.840
he takes some things to a collector named Agatha.&nbsp;
Among the unique items is an unusual table.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.460
"It's a seance table."
"Like Ouija board seance?"&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.940
"A bit more serious than that."

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.400
But this is no ordinary seance table – this&nbsp;
one has hidden drawers containing hidden books.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.560
Agatha brings in a man named Roland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.720
who specializes in rare texts. He's ready&nbsp;
to pay $300,000 for the set, but only if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.860
Nick can produce the fourth missing volume&nbsp;
– one that's meant to bind a demon to Earth.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.140
As they go back to find the text, Roland reveals&nbsp;
the story of the original owner of lot 36 — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.860
German man who made the horrific choice to&nbsp;
offer his sister, Dottie, as a vessel for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.300
a demon. The men discover a hidden room within&nbsp;
the locker that holds Dottie's decaying corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.160
containing a demon in her hollowed-out face.&nbsp;
Nick spots the fourth book just out of reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.000
and though Roland warns him not to touch&nbsp;
it, he cannot resist, thus breaking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.040
spell. Dottie rises as the demon's tentacles&nbsp;
reach out of her face, and Roland is devoured.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.620
Set in Salem at the beginning of the 20th century,&nbsp;
the "Graveyard Rats" story follows Masson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.360
a gravedigger who turned grave robber to pay&nbsp;
off some of his debts. After coming up short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.080
he's convinced that an unlikely suspect&nbsp;
— rats — are stealing the bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.700
along with the valuables. And then, one night,&nbsp;
he has a creepy vision — seeing a rat tail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.300
hanging from his water-stained ceiling,&nbsp;
he gives it a yank and the ceiling opens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.580
raining rats all over him. Later, unearthing a&nbsp;
grave, Masson finds an empty coffin, and then sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.200
the body being dragged away by rats. Determined to&nbsp;
retrieve his fortune, he follows it underground.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.520
" Where are you? I will have my vengeance!"

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.540
Soon enough, Masson comes face to&nbsp;
face with an enormous Rat Queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.220
looming with drool dripping from her razor-sharp&nbsp;
teeth. Determined to retrieve the valuables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.520
he goes on, crawling through tunnels,&nbsp;
battling the Rat Queen and her babies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.440
and even a re-animated skeleton! Making his&nbsp;
way toward what he hopes is the way out, Masson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.660
instead gets trapped in a coffin, trapped forever&nbsp;
by his own greed in classic horror story fashion.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.600
Academy award-winning actor F. Murray Abraham&nbsp;
plays Carl, a medical examiner dying of cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.100
His friend, Sheriff Craven, played by Glynn&nbsp;
Turman, asks for his help explaining some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.900
mysterious deaths that occurred in the local mine.&nbsp;
As Carl performs autopsies on the dead miners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.680
an alien parasite that's taken over one&nbsp;
of the miner's bodies makes itself known.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.240
"What are you?"

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.320
"I'm a traveler."

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.180
Tying Carl to an autopsy table, the alien&nbsp;
creature prepares to transfer itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.840
from the miner's dead body into Carl's&nbsp;
living one. While cutting itself open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.740
the alien explains to Carl his plan to&nbsp;
murder the Sheriff. Carl figures out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.760
that the creature has no senses of its own,&nbsp;
which is why it needs a human body as host.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.680
Acting fast, Carl grabs the knife from the&nbsp;
alien's dead hand. Still tied to the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.600
the alien, in its small, octopus-like true&nbsp;
form, begins burrowing into Carl. He tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.020
killing the creature, but can't&nbsp;
reach it. In a truly heroic move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.340
Carl decides to sacrifice himself, by&nbsp;
destroying his own body before the alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.080
can use it — slicing his ears and eyes with&nbsp;
a scalpel, saving his friend, and the world.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.000
"The Outside" tells the darkly comedic&nbsp;
tale of Stacy, a socially awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.840
bank teller desperate to fit in with her&nbsp;
co-workers, coveting their beauty, clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.460
and perfect lives. When she's invited to one of&nbsp;
their parties and given a box of expensive lotion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.900
she's thrilled, and proceeds to slather it&nbsp;
all over her face. But a horrible allergic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.880
reaction occurs. Refusing to be deterred by&nbsp;
her red, itchy, peeling skin, Stacy continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.200
her regimen, even as her rash worsens and&nbsp;
her devoted husband Keith begs her to stop.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.700
"What's so funny Stacy?"

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.020
"No, I'm not talking to the TV."

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.700
Devoted to his wife, and supportive of&nbsp;
her taxidermy hobby, Keith is a beacon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.540
of sensibility. Keith tries his best to be&nbsp;
supportive of Stacy and her transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.160
journey, even as her skin and mental state get&nbsp;
worse. Finally, it all proves to be too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.480
even for him, and he insists that she stop.&nbsp;
It's a suggestion she doesn't appreciate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.660
to say the least. After killing her&nbsp;
husband, and stuffing poor Keith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.220
like one of her taxidermy projects, her&nbsp;
sought-after makeover is finally complete.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.040
Inspired by an H.P. Lovecraft story, "Pickman's&nbsp;
Model" tells the tale of Richard Pickman, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.300
by Crispin Glover, who invites a young artist&nbsp;
named William Thurber, played by Ben Barnes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.140
to look at his extremely creepy paintings.&nbsp;
One of the paintings depicts a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.160
a distant relative and witch who killed her&nbsp;
husband and served him to members of her coven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.540
Pickman taunts Thurber with the painting, and&nbsp;
Thurber feels deeply disturbed looking at it.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.160
Years later, Thurber continues to be haunted&nbsp;
by Pickman's work, plagued by vivid nightmares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.760
including one in which he walks into the&nbsp;
dinner party from the painting, but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.320
own head is being served as the main course.&nbsp;
Jumping forward in time, we now see Thurber as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.300
an older man with a wife and son, running his own&nbsp;
museum, but still haunted by memories of Pickman.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.820
One day, Pickman unexpectedly returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.300
shocking Thurber. He asks Thurber&nbsp;
to come to his house to see some new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.380
paintings. In an attempt to save others from&nbsp;
the madness he's suffered, Thurber goes alone.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:16.320
"I know you want to see my new paintings! The&nbsp;
finest I've done, I need you to see them."

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.940
But seeing Pickman's new paintings overwhelms&nbsp;
Thurber. Unable to control his rage and anguish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.020
after all the years of torment, he shoots&nbsp;
Pickman and burns down his house. The next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.100
when Thurber takes his family to his museum, he's&nbsp;
shocked to see Pickman's paintings hanging there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.940
and horrifyingly, one of Thurber's colleagues,&nbsp;
Joe, standing in front of the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.740
his eyes gouged out. His wife and child are also&nbsp;
looking at a painting, and he makes them stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.180
but it is too late. When Thurber returns&nbsp;
home, his wife has gouged her eyes out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.960
Most horrifyingly, she's also in the middle&nbsp;
of preparing their son's head for dinner.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.120
Another H.P. Lovecraft inspired story,&nbsp;
this dark fairy tale is brought to life by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.080
Catherine Hardwicke, director of the original&nbsp;
"Twilight." It tells the story of Walter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.520
played by Rupert Grint, of "Harry Potter" fame,&nbsp;
a man obsessed with bringing his twin sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.540
who died when they were young, back to life.&nbsp;
He becomes part of the Spiritualist Society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.140
and discovers a potion that allows him to&nbsp;
find his sister in a place called The Realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.460
of the Dead. Walter even manages to bring her&nbsp;
back to the land of the living, temporarily.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.000
"You brought her back with&nbsp;
you? You left a ripple?"

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.540
"I just wanted my sister back."

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:23.880
Walter's actions anger a witch named Keziah — a&nbsp;
sinister being who also resides in the underworld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.440
and wants Walter's sister to stay there. With&nbsp;
skin like petrified wood and branch-like hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:33.360
she resembles a gnarled tree in human form.&nbsp;
Her screams sound like nails on a chalkboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.100
and her hair is burning embers. Furious, this&nbsp;
witch tries to stab poor Walter in the eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.840
but his ghost sister saves him. She seems&nbsp;
different, now, calmer, telling Walter she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.400
not scared anymore. By the end of the episode,&nbsp;
poor Walter can't really say the same for himself.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.460
Peter Weller, the actor perhaps most famously&nbsp;
known as Robocop, stars in "The Viewing" as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.000
Lionel, a wealthy and eccentric elderly&nbsp;
man who invites four interesting people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.260
to his mansion. There's a psychic named&nbsp;
Targ, an astrophysicist named Charlotte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:06.120
a musician named Randall, and a famous&nbsp;
novelist named Guy. Set in the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.860
"The Viewing" benefits from a very distinctive&nbsp;
70s aesthetic, matching this story's slow burn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.300
intensity. As the plot unfolds, it becomes clear&nbsp;
why Lionel has gathered these people together.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.560
As it turns out, Lionel needs their help&nbsp;
understanding an artifact he's kept tucked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.180
away for years, a strangely unsettling object&nbsp;
that he suspects could be an alien life form.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.080
"Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:31.800
you see that?"

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.780
Eventually, the group awakens the artifact, and&nbsp;
as the meteor-like rock glows and cracks open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.580
a gooey creature with worm-like tendrils&nbsp;
emerges. These tendrils turn into demonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.960
horns, releasing a terrible energy and a&nbsp;
high-pitched sound. Horribly, Targ's' face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:50.400
melts off and Guy's head explodes, which allows&nbsp;
the other two, namely, Charlotte and Randall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.420
to get the heck out of there before they meet&nbsp;
their ends as well. But with the alien unleashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.200
into the world by the end of the night, it's&nbsp;
not exactly what you could call a happy ending.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:03.900
In a tale of quiet grief and haunted houses,&nbsp;
married couple and ornithologists Nancy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.740
Edgar are grieving the loss of their daughter.&nbsp;
But instead of dealing with their devastation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.120
in a healthy manner, they throw themselves&nbsp;
further into their work, which is studying birds.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.400
Starring Andrew Lincoln as Edgar, and Essie Davis&nbsp;
as Nancy, the episode follows the couple as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.380
move into an old house on an isolated island.&nbsp;
Eventually, Nancy starts feeling and seeing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.920
mysterious presence in the house, discovering&nbsp;
through photos and letters that the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.820
occupant, a woman named Claudette, had&nbsp;
suffered many tragedies in the home.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:31.860
"I'm so cold"

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:32.820
"Who's that?"

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.780
Apparently, after Claudette learned that&nbsp;
her husband was married to another woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:41.100
she also discovered she was pregnant. The&nbsp;
husband abandoned her and their unborn child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.120
who remained hidden away in the creepy,&nbsp;
isolated home. Then the ghostly sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.100
and sightings begin. Once, Nancy hears a&nbsp;
child crying and sees a little boy standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.700
at the top of the stairs, dripping wet.&nbsp;
After kneeling down to comfort the child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:59.640
he turns to reveal a decaying and water-bloated&nbsp;
face. The shock knocks Nancy to the floor. Nancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.940
finally asks the groundskeeper about&nbsp;
Claudette, and he tells her that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.140
accidentally killed her son, drowning him in&nbsp;
the bathtub, after which she killed herself.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.640
"How did he die?"

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.100
"He drowned."

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.560
"She drowned him didn't she?"

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:18.720
By the end of "The Murmuring," it seems that&nbsp;
the biggest monster anyone could face is grief.

